1
00:00:02,415 --> 00:00:04,287
A hundred rockets.

2
00:00:04,567 --> 00:00:07,735
Generation one: ninety-nine of them crash.

3
00:00:08,015 --> 00:00:12,239
They fly into walls, off the screen, nowhere near the target.

4
00:00:12,519 --> 00:00:14,391
Now watch.

5
00:00:14,671 --> 00:00:19,351
Generation six: the first rocket slips through the gap and hits the target.

6
00:00:19,631 --> 00:00:22,199
Generation eighty: most of them make it.

7
00:00:22,479 --> 00:00:24,351
And here's the part I love.

8
00:00:24,631 --> 00:00:26,503
Nobody programmed this route.

9
00:00:26,783 --> 00:00:28,655
There's no pathfinding.

10
00:00:28,935 --> 00:00:30,807
No map.

11
00:00:31,087 --> 00:00:34,783
Just random thrusts, a score, and a bit of evolution.

12
00:00:35,063 --> 00:00:40,751
I built this with Claude Code, and in the next ten minutes I'll show you every line that makes it work.

13
00:00:41,551 --> 00:00:50,503
Genetic algorithms are one of the oldest ideas in AI, and they are still used for things like designing antennas, scheduling, and tuning parameters.

14
00:00:50,783 --> 00:00:53,231
The idea is borrowed from biology.

15
00:00:53,511 --> 00:00:55,455
You don't need to understand a problem.

16
00:00:55,735 --> 00:00:57,751
You just need a way to score a solution.

17
00:00:58,031 --> 00:01:02,879
Then you keep the better ones, combine them, add random changes, and repeat.

18
00:01:03,159 --> 00:01:06,255
Compare that with the neural networks we usually talk about.

19
00:01:06,535 --> 00:01:10,663
They need gradients, which means a smooth, differentiable problem.

20
00:01:10,943 --> 00:01:13,535
Evolution works on anything you can score.

21
00:01:13,815 --> 00:01:16,983
Even a rocket flight path through a gap in a wall.

22
00:01:17,783 --> 00:01:20,039
First, what is a rocket's DNA?

23
00:01:20,319 --> 00:01:25,095
It's an array of shape, population by one hundred forty time steps by two.

24
00:01:25,375 --> 00:01:29,191
At each tick, the rocket gets a little push in x and y.

25
00:01:29,471 --> 00:01:32,927
That's the entire genome: a hundred and forty thrusts.

26
00:01:33,207 --> 00:01:35,823
The physics is almost embarrassingly simple.

27
00:01:36,103 --> 00:01:40,207
Add the thrust to velocity, cap the speed, move the rocket.

28
00:01:40,487 --> 00:01:44,543
And because it's numpy, all hundred rockets are simulated at once.

29
00:01:44,823 --> 00:01:48,207
There's no loop over rockets, just a loop over time.

30
00:01:48,487 --> 00:01:54,295
A rocket dies if it leaves the box, enters an obstacle rectangle, or reaches the target.

31
00:01:54,575 --> 00:01:56,807
Now the important part: fitness.

32
00:01:57,087 --> 00:01:59,103
How do you score a rocket that crashed?.

33
00:01:59,383 --> 00:02:07,303
The score is based on the closest the rocket ever got to the target, so rockets that crashed near the goal still beat rockets that wandered off.

34
00:02:07,583 --> 00:02:10,487
Crashing costs sixty percent of the score.

35
00:02:10,767 --> 00:02:16,311
And if you reach the target, you jump to over two hundred, plus a bonus for arriving earlier.

36
00:02:16,591 --> 00:02:21,103
That bonus is why later generations get faster, not just successful.

37
00:02:21,903 --> 00:02:23,775
Now the evolution step.

38
00:02:24,055 --> 00:02:28,135
Each generation: simulate, score, sort by fitness.

39
00:02:28,415 --> 00:02:30,431
Then build the next population..

40
00:02:30,711 --> 00:02:32,583
Line by line.

41
00:02:32,863 --> 00:02:38,647
Elitism: the top ten percent are copied unchanged, so we never lose our best rocket.

42
00:02:38,927 --> 00:02:42,647
Selection: parents are drawn at random from the top half.

43
00:02:42,927 --> 00:02:50,247
Crossover: pick a random cut point; the child takes its first thrusts from parent A and the rest from parent B.

44
00:02:50,527 --> 00:02:56,383
That's the np.where line, with the cut point broadcast across the whole population at once.

45
00:02:56,663 --> 00:03:05,279
Mutation: each thrust has a small chance, the mutation rate, default two percent, of being replaced with a fresh random direction.

46
00:03:05,559 --> 00:03:08,127
Finally stick elites and children together.

47
00:03:08,407 --> 00:03:10,039
That's the next generation.

48
00:03:10,319 --> 00:03:12,191
That's the whole thing.

49
00:03:12,471 --> 00:03:18,999
I also store every generation's paths, sorted best first, which is why the UI can scrub through them.

50
00:03:19,279 --> 00:03:28,351
One detail I like: the initial DNA is random directions of length point three five, so the first generation is basically a random walk.

51
00:03:28,631 --> 00:03:33,526
The first rocket to touch the target in my default run needs six generations.

52
00:03:34,327 --> 00:03:36,199
Here's the app.

53
00:03:36,478 --> 00:03:38,351
Level: Gap.

54
00:03:38,630 --> 00:03:44,270
Population a hundred, eighty generations, mutation point oh two, seed one.

55
00:03:44,550 --> 00:03:47,694
It evolves everything upfront, then I can scrub.

56
00:03:47,974 --> 00:03:49,846
Generation one.

57
00:03:50,126 --> 00:03:51,998
Ninety-nine crashed.

58
00:03:52,278 --> 00:03:57,102
The faint blue lines are the population, the thick orange one is the best rocket.

59
00:03:57,382 --> 00:03:59,014
It's going nowhere useful.

60
00:03:59,294 --> 00:04:01,358
I drag the generation slider.

61
00:04:01,638 --> 00:04:03,606
The cloud starts to lean upward.

62
00:04:03,886 --> 00:04:13,390
Around generation six the blue info box says "First rocket reached the target in generation six." Keep going and the cloud funnels through the gap.

63
00:04:13,670 --> 00:04:25,046
Generation eighty: seventy-eight reached the target, twenty-two crashed, and the fitness chart on the right shows the best score climbing to around two hundred ninety-eight while the average catches up behind it.

64
00:04:25,326 --> 00:04:27,198
Now change the level.

65
00:04:27,478 --> 00:04:31,150
Open sky: the first success is at generation two.

66
00:04:31,430 --> 00:04:33,302
Wall: six.

67
00:04:33,582 --> 00:04:35,454
Gap: six.

68
00:04:35,734 --> 00:04:45,526
Zig-zag, the hard one with two staggered walls: first success is generation eleven, and by generation eighty only forty-one of a hundred reach it.

69
00:04:45,806 --> 00:04:49,646
Those numbers are from my runs with the default settings and seed one.

70
00:04:49,926 --> 00:04:51,894
Change the seed and they move.

71
00:04:52,694 --> 00:04:56,222
What surprised me was how fast it goes from nothing to something.

72
00:04:56,502 --> 00:05:03,726
The best fitness in generation one is below one, and then once a rocket reaches the target it jumps past two hundred.

73
00:05:04,006 --> 00:05:09,358
That jump comes from the reach bonus, which makes success worth far more than getting close.

74
00:05:09,638 --> 00:05:15,518
Second, the mutation rate matters in a way that's very visible, and not the way I expected.

75
00:05:15,798 --> 00:05:28,998
On the Gap level, with three seeds each, point oh oh five had eighty-eight to ninety-two rockets arriving at generation eighty, the default point oh two had seventy to seventy-eight, and point one had only twenty-one to forty.

76
00:05:29,278 --> 00:05:32,326
Too much mutation keeps scrambling a good route.

77
00:05:32,606 --> 00:05:40,886
But on Zig-zag, the low rate got stuck on one seed, never reaching the target at all, so a little mutation helps when the problem is hard.

78
00:05:41,166 --> 00:05:43,614
Play with that slider rather than trusting me.

79
00:05:43,894 --> 00:05:46,126
Third: the rockets don't plan.

80
00:05:46,406 --> 00:05:48,998
They just replay a fixed list of thrusts.

81
00:05:49,278 --> 00:05:54,870
If I moved the wall by a few pixels, the DNA would be wrong and they'd need to evolve again.

82
00:05:55,670 --> 00:05:57,542
Honest limits.

83
00:05:57,822 --> 00:05:59,694
This is a toy.

84
00:05:59,974 --> 00:06:04,942
The DNA is a fixed-length open-loop script, so the rocket doesn't sense anything.

85
00:06:05,222 --> 00:06:13,550
The Zig-zag level is hard, and on some seeds it doesn't get solved in eighty generations; the app tells you to try more generations.

86
00:06:13,830 --> 00:06:20,094
Everything is one run with one seed, so I'd treat my numbers as examples, not benchmarks.

87
00:06:20,374 --> 00:06:23,014
And I haven't compared this to smarter methods.

88
00:06:23,294 --> 00:06:26,798
A real controller would use sensors and a neural network.

89
00:06:27,078 --> 00:06:34,182
Also, I built this with Claude Code, and I verified the generation numbers by running the app's own functions headlessly.

90
00:06:34,982 --> 00:06:41,318
Next I want to evolve a neural network instead of a list of thrusts, so rockets can react to obstacles.

91
00:06:41,598 --> 00:06:43,398
Or add moving obstacles.

92
00:06:43,678 --> 00:06:45,886
Or let you draw the walls with the mouse.

93
00:06:46,166 --> 00:06:51,614
The code is in the description, it needs no API key and costs nothing to run.

94
00:06:51,894 --> 00:06:57,174
If you enjoyed this, subscribe, there are sixteen of these AI and ML builds.

95
00:06:57,454 --> 00:06:59,902
And in the comments: what would you evolve?

96
00:07:00,182 --> 00:07:04,670
The narration is AI-generated, and the app was built with Claude Code.

97
00:07:04,950 --> 00:07:06,822
Thanks for watching.
